export const body = "<p>In the article about \"Cover,\" we briefly mentioned some examples of volatile shelters, which degrade with firepower. Going a bit further, we can find games that even allow for large-scale destruction, granting players the ability to collapse buildings and ravage terrain. These games, by the industry's definition, incorporate destructible environments, ones that can be altered by player behavior, instead of being predetermined by a programmer.</p>\n\n<p>In this article, we'll go back two decades to discover where the first explosion took place, and in the process, see how it has echoed throughout the years.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/c3edb53125dde0c3d8dd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/c3edb53125dde0c3d8dd.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/6b933430c71a7ba32862.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/6b933430c71a7ba32862.webp\" width=\"682\" height=\"381\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/90763ff056d4b08c1c84.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/90763ff056d4b08c1c84.webp\" width=\"682\" height=\"381\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The first explosion took place in 2001, in a video game called <em>Red Faction</em>. Unlike its contemporaries, <em>Red Faction</em>'s selling point didn't lie in the gunplay but in the environment where the gunplay unfolded. Powered by an engine named \"Geo-Mod,\" abbreviated from \"Geometry Modification,\" the game allowed players to damage their surroundings in various ways, from landing a mine to blow up the ground to blasting a rocket to burst through a wall.</p>\n\n<p>This innovative feature was a novelty that outlived the lukewarm reception <em>Red Faction</em> faced in its initial release. Drawing similarity with <em>Kill Switch</em>, the cover-shooter pioneer, <em>Red Faction</em> left a seminal imprint in the game industry long after it disappeared from public consciousness.</p>\n\n<p>Following the resounding success of <em>Half-Life</em> in 1998, the development team set out to create a sequel that would again redefine the genre. While the first game aimed to interweave strategic gunplay with a cinematic narrative, the second game focused on expanding the level of interaction the game world lends to players. As a result, the developers revamped their engine to develop a detailed physics simulation for <em>Half-Life 2</em>.</p>\n\n<p>When <em>Half-Life 2 </em>was released in 2004, it immediately became a classic, and its physics system is still one of the most well-realized to this day. In terms of destructibility, wood is the most prominent material, making up the architecture and furniture of the game. Timber planks fracture and fall realistically, wooden furniture shatter upon receiving heavy damage. Such details not only enhance the immersion but also the gameplay since players could alter their surroundings to great effect.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/4d1e0df340f29650c4f8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/4d1e0df340f29650c4f8.webp\" width=\"989\" height=\"700\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/65adad46ba51fefec25a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/65adad46ba51fefec25a.webp\" width=\"682\" height=\"381\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/9fbb82015ec530c6a36d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/9fbb82015ec530c6a36d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"645\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/da7ef09b24ee84dbdf7d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/da7ef09b24ee84dbdf7d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Destructible woodwork has since become a staple in video games. Whether it is a door or a table, timber-made objects are prone to destruction. Other than a heightened sense of realism, destructible environments also entail more dynamic gameplay scenarios. For example, <em>Resident Evil 4</em>, released just one year after <em>Half-Life 2</em>, built almost every door out of timber. Because of that, the player cannot rely on doors to deny access to their enemies, which adds tension to combat in tight interior spaces.</p>\n\n<p>And as mentioned last time in the \"Cover\" article, <em>Uncharted 4</em> boasts a wide range of environments, resulting in a rich assortment of cover objects. This decision opened up the opportunity to include destructible cover in the game when it's appropriate, which in turn helped construct gunfight arenas that pressed the player to move from place to place.</p>\n\n<p><em>Battlefield Bad Company 2</em> was the first entry in the franchise to implement destructible environments. The game placed a heavy emphasis on this feature, to the point where you can demolish an entire building. Since the majority of the levels are destructible, players have to opt for a more aggressive playstyle rather than the hide-and-shoot method.</p>\n\n<p>This feature had been brought back for subsequent installments, most recently<em> Battlefield 1</em> and <em>Battlefield V</em>. However, the level of destruction was toned down. For balancing purposes, several parts of the environment are invulnerable to provide permanent cover. In the opposite picture, only the front of the building can be crushed; the sides and the furniture are immune to all kinds of deterioration.</p>\n\n<p><sub><em>Note: As of this article, Battlefield 2042 hasn't been released.</em></sub></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/23ff32639f21b24a205e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/23ff32639f21b24a205e.webp\" width=\"1048\" height=\"333\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/91ebe2178dd3213ad068.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/91ebe2178dd3213ad068.webp\" width=\"1047\" height=\"283\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/f83184ef521829ae5470.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/f83184ef521829ae5470.webp\" width=\"1048\" height=\"279\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Speaking of balance, <em>Rainbow Six Siege </em>is a team-based tactical shooter built around destructible environments. The game devises levels that are primarily indoor, asking opposing teams to flank and assault one another by breaking walls and floors. This design idea influenced other aspects of the game, from the tuning of firearms to the asset creation. Different guns damage the environments to different degrees: a pistol cannot blow a hole in a wall as effectively as a shotgun, for example. As the wall is disintegrated, its inner frame is revealed, and depending on the material, the frame may or may not be exempt from destruction. Timber frames are destructible, metal frames indestructible.</p>\n\n<p>Finally, let's have a look at <em>Teardown, </em>a pixel-precise destruction simulator. Instead of polygons, the game is wholly comprised of 3D grids called \"voxels,\" which is a vast topic (that we'll probably cover later). For now, you can think of it as pixels but 3D. With this system, <em>Teardown </em>is the exact collisions of objects, down to the individual bits and pieces.</p>";
export const images = ["assets/concept-inline/23ff32639f21b24a205e.webp", "assets/concept-inline/4d1e0df340f29650c4f8.webp", "assets/concept-inline/65adad46ba51fefec25a.webp", "assets/concept-inline/6b933430c71a7ba32862.webp", "assets/concept-inline/90763ff056d4b08c1c84.webp", "assets/concept-inline/91ebe2178dd3213ad068.webp", "assets/concept-inline/9fbb82015ec530c6a36d.webp", "assets/concept-inline/c3edb53125dde0c3d8dd.webp", "assets/concept-inline/da7ef09b24ee84dbdf7d.webp", "assets/concept-inline/f83184ef521829ae5470.webp"];
